#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "c44c0973-5253-50c5-98b1-15938b79494e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3054" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+3t\\,\\mathbf j+2t^2\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,3,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf j + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j + z(t)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,3,2)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + 3j + 2k \\\\\ni + 3tj + 2t^{2}k &= i + 3j + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above equation, the first component in each of the two vectors is equal to $1$. Equating the other two components gives the system\n\n$$\n{3t = 3; 2t^{2} = 2\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $t=1$. This value satisfies the second equation too. Therefore, the point $P(1,3,2)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{2})k \\\\\n&= 0i + 3j + 4tk \\\\\n&= 3j + 4tk \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= 3j + 4(1)k \\\\\n&= 3j + 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,3,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= (r^{′}(1))/(‖r^{′}(1)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3j + 4k}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}j + \\frac{4}{5}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "95ba9167-9e49-557d-bfbd-89bdeec8939f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3055" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sin^3{t}, \\:\\cos^3{t} \\right\\rangle$ at an arbitrary point where $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf i + y(t) \\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin^{3} t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos^{3} t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin^{2} t\\cos t, 3\\cos^{2} t(-\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= 3\\sin t\\cos t⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\begin{vmatrix}3\\sin t\\cos t & \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + (-\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\ = & 3\\sin t\\cos t\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\mid 3\\sin t\\cos t \\mid \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 3\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= (r^{′}(t))/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sin t\\cos t⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩}{3\\sin t\\cos t} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sin t,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bb704e8f-1fab-50c0-b768-c50c729d4e45" :question/math-academy-id "e-3056" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k$." :question/worked-solution "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t}p(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t})p(t) + e^{2t}\\frac{d}{dt}(p(t)) \\\\\n&= 2e^{2t}p(t) + e^{2t}p^{′}(t) \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= 2e^{2(0)}p(0) + e^{2(0)}p^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= 2(i - j - k) + 1(i - j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 3i - 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (0)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= (r^{′}(0))/(‖r^{′}(0)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3i - 3j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i - j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i - j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e6a322-bfe3-5866-9e00-ec11064f82f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j + \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117499} {:answer/id #uuid "a8354a80-f3ad-53fb-a079-199fa7ca8aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117500} {:answer/id #uuid "ae30b1c7-8c5a-5859-9ebc-63f23d7e1aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117501} {:answer/id #uuid "0a13a4c6-ec2f-5f19-a257-25fc60c3552c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117502} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb5a3f8-58a4-5acd-869a-bce3409cf5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8354a80-f3ad-53fb-a079-199fa7ca8aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117500} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca42adc8-9c03-57d4-8619-2101dd0bb0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117498}] :question/id #uuid "ecacb100-aa15-51ca-8b52-7438cb895ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33096" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = e^{-t}p(t)$ at the point where $t = 0$, if $p(0) = i + j + k$ and $p^{′}(0) = i - j$."}]}
